    "https://external-jobs.csu.edu.au/cw/en/job/499211/executive-assistant": {
      "title": "Executive Assistant",
      "description": "Executive Assistant Apply now Reference no: 499211 Work type: Continuing - Part Time Location: Wagga Wagga Categories: Administration, Professional Enjoy a rare part-time, continuing opportunity with generous university staff benefits Work closely with the Executive Dean to coordinate priorities and support faculty leadership Build strong connections across the faculty and wider University The Role Continuing, Part-time - 40% fraction (2 days a week) Job share arrangement - Monday and Tuesday preferred Level 5 - $32,799 - $35,808 pa (plus 17% superannuation) Wagga Wagga (On-site, campus-based role) As Executive Assistant, you will provide high-level support to the Executive Dean and help coordinate priorities across the Faculty of Science and Health Office. You will manage diary commitments, correspondence and travel, provide committee support, and coordinate key staff processes, events and faculty projects. Your organisation, initiative and discretion will help faculty leaders stay informed, meet commitments and progress their priorities. As a key contact for faculty enquiries, you will build strong relationships across the University and provide professional, responsive service to staff, students and visitors. About you Relevant qualifications or an equivalent combination of education, training and experience Experience supporting senior managers, with strong office administration skills, including diary and travel coordination Strong organisational skills to manage competing priorities, meet deadlines and maintain attention to detail Clear, confident communication skills, with the ability to negotiate, communicate with influence and provide responsive service to diverse stakeholders Initiative and sound judgement to solve problems, handle confidential information with discretion and work independently and collaboratively About Us We are a university of the land and people of our regions. True to the character of regional Australia we have gumption, we have soul and we collaborate with others. We develop holistic, far-sighted people who help their communities grow and flourish. The Wiradjuri phrase yindyamarra winhanganha means the wisdom of respectfully knowing how to live well in a world worth living in. This phrase represents who we are at Charles Sturt University – our ethos. It comes from traditional Indigenous Australian knowledge, but it also speaks to the mission of universities – to develop and spread wisdom to make the world a better place. Harnessing technology, we thrive as a distributed yet connected community, welcoming and engaging with people across Australia and the world. Learn more about the Faculty of Science and Health here . Visit our website to learn about our great range of employee benefits . Charles Sturt University is committed to building a diverse workforce as shown through our Athena Swan Bronze Award, our Australian Workplace Equality Index Bronze Award and our membership of a range of diversity focused organisations including Diversity Council Australia and Pride in Diversity. We encourage applications from First Nations people, carers, people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, people with disability, neurodivergent people, and people of all age groups, genders and sexualities for all roles. To apply Click the Apply button and complete your application online, and address the selection criteria in the position description and upload your CV. If you experience difficulties applying online or for further information on completing the application process please visit our how to apply page or contact us . This position is open to Australian Citizens and Permanent Residents; or applicants who hold a current valid work visa commensurate with this position. Disclosure Requirement Applicants may be required to disclose information relating to gender-based violence allegations or findings as part of the University's recruitment and screening processes. Further Information Additional information is available in the position description or by contacting: Peter Ryall | Faculty Executive Officer | pryall@csu.edu.au | Ph: (02) 6933 2863 Closing Date: 11pm, 10 November 2026 Advertised: 27 Oct 2026 09:00 AM - AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 10 Nov 2026 11:00 PM - AUS Eastern Daylight Time Apply now Refer a friend Back to search results"
    },
    "https://external-jobs.csu.edu.au/cw/en/job/499185/head-school-of-computing-mathematics-and-engineering": {
      "title": "Head, School of Computing, Mathematics and Engineering",
      "description": "Head, School of Computing, Mathematics and Engineering Apply now Reference no: 499185 Work type: Fixed Term - Full Time Location: Bathurst, Port Macquarie, Wagga Wagga Categories: Executive, Academic - Leadership Shape the future of STEM education, research and innovation. Lead a high-performing school with national influence and impact. Join Australia's leading regional university and inspire excellence. The role Full Time, Fixed Term (up to 5 years) Senior Leader, Level 4 Remuneration package starting from $208,921 pa (including superannuation) Bathurst, Port Macquarie or Wagga Wagga You are a contemporary academic leader with a strong track record of delivering results in complex higher education environments. Combining strategic vision with operational excellence, you have successfully led people, resources and change while creating a culture of accountability, collaboration and continuous improvement. You bring a doctoral qualification in a discipline relevant to computing, information technology, mathematics or engineering, together with significant leadership experience in a multidisciplinary university environment. You have demonstrated success in improving teaching quality and student outcomes, building research capability and impact, managing significant budgets and resources, and developing high-performing teams. Equally comfortable engaging with academics, students, industry, government and professional bodies, you are recognised for your ability to build strong partnerships, influence outcomes and inspire others around a shared vision. Most importantly, you are committed to creating an inclusive and supportive environment that reflects the values of Charles Sturt University and supports excellence in learning, research and engagement. About you You are an accomplished academic leader with a track record of delivering excellence in teaching, research and engagement. You combine strategic thinking with strong people leadership and are passionate about creating positive outcomes for students, staff and industry. You will bring: A doctoral qualification in a relevant discipline Significant leadership experience in higher education or a comparable environment A proven ability to lead and develop high-performing teams Experience driving excellence in learning, teaching and research Strong financial, operational and resource management capabilities Exceptional stakeholder engagement and partnership-building skills The ability to lead change, influence outcomes and deliver strategic results A commitment to fostering an inclusive, collaborative and high-performing culture. About us We are a university of the land and people of our regions. True to the character of regional Australia we have gumption, we have soul and we collaborate with others. We develop holistic, far-sighted people who help their communities grow and flourish. The Wiradjuri phrase yindyamarra winhanganha means the wisdom of respectfully knowing how to live well in a world worth living in. This phrase represents who we are at Charles Sturt University – our ethos. It comes from traditional Indigenous Australian knowledge, but it also speaks to the mission of universities – to develop and spread wisdom to make the world a better place. Harnessing technology, we thrive as a distributed yet connected community, welcoming and engaging with people across Australia and the world. Learn more about the School of Computing, Mathematics and Engineering here . Visit our website to learn about our great range of employee benefits . Charles Sturt University is committed to building a diverse workforce as shown through our Athena Swan Bronze Award, our Australian Workplace Equality Index Bronze Award and our membership of a range of diversity focused organisations including Diversity Council Australia and Pride in Diversity. We encourage applications from First Nations people, carers, people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, people with disability, neurodivergent people, and people of all age groups, genders and sexualities for all roles. To apply Click the Apply button and complete your application online, and address the selection criteria in the position description and upload your CV. If you experience difficulties applying online or for further information on completing the application process please visit our how to apply page or contact us . This position is open to Australian Citizens and Permanent Residents; or applicants who hold a current valid work visa commensurate with this position. Disclosure Requirement Applicants may be required to disclose information relating to gender-based violence allegations or findings as part of the University's recruitment and screening processes. Further information Additional information is available in the Applicant Information Brochure or by contacting: Recruitment specific enquiries: Rebecca Dwyer | Manager, Talent Acquisition | redwyer@csu.edu.au Job specific enquiries: Professor Lewis Bizo | Executive Dean, Faculty of Business, Justice and Behavioural Sciences | lbizo@csu.edu.au Closing Date: 11pm, 7 December 2026 Advertised: 26 Oct 2026 09:00 AM - AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 07 Dec 2026 11:00 PM - AUS Eastern Daylight Time Apply now Refer a friend Back to search results"
    },
    "https://external-jobs.csu.edu.au/cw/en/job/499165/postdoctoral-research-fellow-functional-biomaterials-for-sustainable-agriculture": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow - Functional Biomaterials for Sustainable Agriculture",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow - Functional Biomaterials for Sustainable Agriculture Apply now Reference no: 499165 Work type: Fixed Term - Full Time Location: Wagga Wagga Categories: Academic - Research Only/Fellowship Drive research that turns agricultural waste into sustainable solutions Work with industry on innovative biomaterials and agricultural products Make an impact across research, sustainability and commercialisation The role Full Time, Fixed Term (up to 12 months) Level A - $80,046 to $108,204 pa (plus 17% superannuation) Wagga Wagga As a Postdoctoral Research Fellow, you will support an ARC Mid-Career Industry Fellowship project focused on transforming agricultural waste into low-cost, high-quality substrates and soil amendment products. You will develop controlled-release biomaterials designed to improve nutrient release and water retention for mushroom cultivation and crop growth. Working with industry partners and a multidisciplinary research team, you will plan and deliver experimental research spanning nanomaterials synthesis and characterisation, laboratory studies, plant and soil experiments, data analysis and research translation. You will contribute to publications, reports and project milestones while supporting the scale-up, commercialisation and practical application of research outcomes. About you A PhD in materials science, chemistry, chemical engineering, nanotechnology, agricultural science, agronomy, soil science or a related discipline. Research experience in areas such as agricultural-waste valorisation, mushroom cultivation, substrate development, nutrient-release studies or plant and soil experiments. Demonstrated ability to design rigorous experiments, analyse quantitative data and maintain reproducible research records. A strong record of research outputs, with high-level scientific writing, presentation and collaboration skills. Ability to work independently and collaboratively, manage competing priorities and build productive relationships with researchers and industry partners. You will bring curiosity, innovation and a collaborative approach to applied research, with the ability to translate scientific ideas into practical outcomes. Experience in nanomaterials, scale-up, industry validation, commercialisation or analytical techniques such as microscopy, spectroscopy or nutrient-release assays will be highly regarded. About us We are a university of the land and people of our regions. True to the character of regional Australia we have gumption, we have soul and we collaborate with others. We develop holistic, far-sighted people who help their communities grow and flourish. The Wiradjuri phrase yindyamarra winhanganha means the wisdom of respectfully knowing how to live well in a world worth living in. This phrase represents who we are at Charles Sturt University – our ethos. It comes from traditional Indigenous Australian knowledge, but it also speaks to the mission of universities – to develop and spread wisdom to make the world a better place. Harnessing technology, we thrive as a distributed yet connected community, welcoming and engaging with people across Australia and the world. Learn more about the Gulbali Institute here . Visit our website to learn about our great range of employee benefits . To apply Click the Apply button and complete your application online, and address the selection criteria in the position description and upload your CV. If you experience difficulties applying online or for further information on completing the application process please visit our how to apply page or contact us . This position is open to Australian Citizens and Permanent Residents; or applicants who hold a current valid work visa commensurate with this position. Disclosure Requirement Applicants may be required to disclose information relating to gender-based violence allegations or findings as part of the University's recruitment and screening processes. Further information Additional information is available in the position description or by contacting: Job specific enquiries: Li Li | Principal Research Fellow of Advanced Biomineral Nanomaterials | lili@csu.edu.au Closing Date: 11pm, 10 November 2026 Advertised: 20 Oct 2026 09:00 AM - AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 10 Nov 2026 11:00 PM - AUS Eastern Daylight Time Apply now Refer a friend Back to search results"
    },
    "https://external-jobs.csu.edu.au/cw/en/job/499206/chief-financial-officer": {
      "title": "Chief Financial Officer",
      "description": "Chief Financial Officer Reference no: 499206 Work type: Fixed Term - Full Time Location: Albury-Wodonga, Bathurst, Dubbo, Orange, Port Macquarie, Wagga Wagga Categories: Finance, Executive We are a university deeply connected to the land, people and communities of regional Australia. We develop capable, forward-thinking graduates and professionals who create meaningful impact, helping their communities thrive today and into the future. Through strong regional foundations and enduring partnerships, we build connections that extend across Australia and resonate globally. The Wiradjuri phrase yindyamarra winhanganha means \"the wisdom of respectfully knowing how to live well in a world worth living in\". It lies at the heart of Charles Sturt University and shapes our values, culture and purpose. Grounded in traditional Indigenous knowledge, it also reflects the enduring role of universities: to create, share and apply knowledge that improves lives and contributes to a better future. As a distributed yet connected university, we harness technology and innovation to bring people together across locations, communities and disciplines. Our ambition is to be Australia's leading regional university, renowned for research excellence and for delivering regional outcomes with global impact. Reporting to the Chief Operating Officer, The Chief Financial Officer (CFO) is a key executive leadership role responsible for shaping the University's long-term financial sustainability, revenue strategy and broader institutional direction. The CFO will lead a significant program of finance transformation and process improvement, driving contemporary financial practices, system enhancements and operational efficiencies across a complex and geographically dispersed organisation. The CFO will provide high-quality financial analysis, advice and strategic options to support University-wide decision-making. The role will engage confidently with key internal and external stakeholders, while also supporting the Vice-Chancellor, Executive Leadership Team and the University Council on finance-related matters and presentations. The successful candidate will bring a proven track record of leading finance-system transformation and delivering genuine operational efficiencies. They will combine strong financial and commercial acumen with the ability to act as an effective enterprise business partner. Experience working across complex, multi-site or geographically dispersed organisations will be important, as will the ability to lead, engage and develop a distributed finance team. Experience in Higher education would be highly regarded, however outstanding candidates from relevant public-sector, service-intensive or other similar / adjacent complex environments will also be considered. The successful candidate will demonstrate sound judgement, clear communication, collaborative leadership and the credibility to influence across an institution. Equally comfortable operating at both strategic and operational levels, they will play a critical role in supporting Charles Sturt University's continued financial strength, sustainability and evolution as a student-centred institution with a strong regional mission and global outlook. Charles Sturt University is committed to building a diverse workforce as shown through our Athena Swan Bronze Award, our Australian Workplace Equality Index Bronze Award and our membership of a range of diversity focused organisations including Diversity Council Australia and Pride in Diversity. We encourage applications from First Nations people, carers, people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, people with disability, neurodivergent people, and people of all age groups, genders and sexualities for all roles. To learn more about this unique opportunity please contact our advising consultants Alun Parry, Andrew Ng and Dr Margot Courtillat at Boyden on +61 2 9225 7400, or margot.courtillat@boyden.com Applications close on 16 November 2026 Advertised: 20 Oct 2026 09:00 AM - AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 16 Nov 2026 11:00 PM - AUS Eastern Daylight Time Refer a friend Back to search results"
    },
    "https://external-jobs.csu.edu.au/cw/en/job/499166/associate-lecturer-lecturer-in-enabling-education": {
      "title": "Associate Lecturer / Lecturer in Enabling Education",
      "description": "Associate Lecturer / Lecturer in Enabling Education Apply now Reference no: 499166 Work type: Continuing - Part Time Location: Bathurst, Port Macquarie, Wagga Wagga Categories: Academic - Teaching Focussed Engage with best practice in the Fee Free Uni Ready environment Teach in an innovative, collegiate team Regionally located role with requirement for on campus teaching The role Part Time – 22.5 hours per week, Continuing Preferred days of work - Tuesday, Wednesday and Thursday Level A - $48,027 to $64,922 pa (plus 17% superannuation) Level B - $68,304 to $80,979 pa (plus 17% superannuation) Bathurst, Port Macquarie or Wagga Wagga At Charles Sturt University, we believe everyone deserves the opportunity to succeed in higher education. Our Enabling and Transition team deliver open access, capacity building subjects which provide diverse students with basis for entry to degree courses. The role is regionally based, and the incumbent will be required to be available to teach face-to-face on the Charles Sturt campus where they are located. The successful applicant will be a passionate and experienced educator with deep understanding of inclusive practice and enabling pedagogies as well as discipline expertise in one or more of the following: academic literacies, mathematics, science and/or TESOL. The role demands high levels of competence with digital technologies and demonstrated experience and/or preparedness to develop skills in curriculum design. This is an opportunity to join a student-centred team committed to best practice in widening participation. You will play a key role in changing higher education outcomes in rural and regional Australia and have impact on an identified strategic growth area in the University. About you A relevant doctoral qualification (Level B) or postgraduate qualification (Level A) or equivalent accreditation and standing. Demonstrated expertise in teaching with impact in one or more of the following: academic literacies, mathematics, science and/or TESOL. Demonstrated competence in current digital teaching technologies and practices with preparedness to innovate and commitment to quality and standards. Experience with working collaboratively and independently in an academic setting. About us We are a university of the land and people of our regions. True to the character of regional Australia we have gumption, we have soul and we collaborate with others. We develop holistic, far-sighted people who help their communities grow and flourish. The Wiradjuri phrase yindyamarra winhanganha means the wisdom of respectfully knowing how to live well in a world worth living in. This phrase represents who we are at Charles Sturt University – our ethos. It comes from traditional Indigenous Australian knowledge, but it also speaks to the mission of universities – to develop and spread wisdom to make the world a better place. Harnessing technology, we thrive as a distributed yet connected community, welcoming and engaging with people across Australia and the world. Learn more about the Division of Student Success here . Visit our website to learn about our great range of employee benefits . Charles Sturt University is committed to building a diverse workforce as shown through our Athena Swan Bronze Award, our Australian Workplace Equality Index Bronze Award and our membership of a range of diversity focused organisations including Diversity Council Australia and Pride in Diversity. We encourage applications from First Nations people, carers, people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, people with disability, neurodivergent people, and people of all age groups, genders and sexualities for all roles. To apply Click the Apply button and complete your application online, submit a “statement of suitability” demonstrating your skills, knowledge and/or experience relevant to the position (referencing the selection criteria in the position description) and upload your CV. If you experience difficulties applying online or for further information on completing the application process please visit our how to apply page or contact us . This position is open to Australian Citizens and Permanent Residents; or applicants who hold a current valid work visa commensurate with this position. Disclosure Requirement Applicants may be required to disclose information relating to gender-based violence allegations or findings as part of the University's recruitment and screening processes. Further information Additional information is available in the position description(s) below: Position Description - Level A - Associate Lecturer in Enabling Education Position Description - Level B - Lecturer in Enabling Education or by contacting: General Enquiries: jobs@csu.edu.au Job specific enquiries: Dr Carolyn O’Dwyer | Program Director (Enabling and Transition) | codwyer@csu.edu.au Closing Date: 11pm, 11 November 2026 Advertised: 28 Oct 2026 09:00 AM - AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 11 Nov 2026 11:00 PM - AUS Eastern Daylight Time Apply now Refer a friend Back to search results"
    },
    "https://external-jobs.csu.edu.au/cw/en/job/499220/adviser-first-nations-students-identified-first-nations-position": {
      "title": "Adviser, First Nations Students - Identified First Nations Position",
      "description": "Adviser, First Nations Students - Identified First Nations Position Apply now Reference no: 499220 Work type: Continuing - Full Time Location: Port Macquarie, Wagga Wagga Categories: Indigenous Identified Grow your career and connect with mob at the university where you belong​ The role Full Time, Continuing Level 6 - $95,246 to $103,022 p.a. (plus 17% superannuation) Port Macquarie and Wagga Wagga Are you passionate about supporting First Nations students to thrive at university and achieve their goals? As an Adviser, First Nations Students, you will play a vital role in creating a positive and culturally safe student experience. Working as part of the First Nations Student Connect team, you'll provide personalised advice, advocacy and support to students throughout their university journey, helping them navigate challenges, access services, build connections, and succeed academically and personally. You'll engage with students individually and through group programs, both on campus and online, building trusted relationships that foster belonging, confidence and success. You'll also collaborate closely with colleagues across the University, as well as families, communities and external organisations, to ensure students receive coordinated and culturally responsive support. This is a rewarding opportunity to make a meaningful difference in the lives of First Nations students while contributing to the University's commitment to student success, equity and inclusion. About you To be successful in this role, you will bring: Experience supporting First Nations peoples using culturally responsive, strengths-based and inclusive approaches. Strong interpersonal and communication skills, with the ability to build trusted relationships and work effectively with students, communities, colleagues and external stakeholders. Well-developed organisational skills, with the ability to manage competing priorities, exercise initiative and deliver high-quality outcomes. A collaborative and student-centred approach, with a genuine commitment to supporting student success and wellbeing. Confidence using technology and learning new systems to deliver efficient and effective services. Appointment will be conditional upon the successful applicant satisfying and maintaining a “Working with Children Check”, in accordance with the Commission for Children and Young People Act (NSW). About us At Charles Sturt University, we deeply value the knowledges, cultures and leadership that First Nations peoples bring. We're committed to building a culturally safe and inclusive workplace where mob feel respected, supported and connected. We're growing our First Nations workforce, with opportunities across teaching, research, student support and other professional roles. Bring your skills, experience and perspectives to meaningful work that supports our students, communities and future generations.​ When you join Charles Sturt, you'll be part of a connected First Nations staff community, with opportunities for professional development, cultural leave and connection through First Nations Staff Connect. We're committed to supporting First Nations careers and creating a workplace where mob feel valued, connected and supported to succeed.​ Learn more about the Division of Student Success here . Visit our website to learn about First Nations careers at Charles Sturt . Charles Sturt University is committed to building a diverse workforce as shown through our Athena Swan Bronze Award, our Australian Workplace Equality Index Bronze Award and our membership of a range of diversity focused organisations including Diversity Council Australia and Pride in Diversity. We encourage applications from First Nations people, carers, people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, people with disability, neurodivergent people, and people of all age groups, genders and sexualities for all roles. This is an identified Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander position under section 14 of the NSW Anti-Discrimination Act, 1977. Please be aware you may be asked to provide confirmation of Indigenous Australian Identity. To apply Click the Apply button and complete your application online, submit a “statement of suitability” demonstrating your skills, knowledge and/or experience relevant to the position (referencing the selection criteria in the position description) and upload your CV. If you experience difficulties applying online or for further information on completing the application process please visit our how to apply page or contact us . Disclosure Requirement Applicants may be required to disclose information relating to gender-based violence allegations or findings as part of the University's recruitment and screening processes. Further information Additional information is available in the position description or by contacting: Blake Dunn | Senior Manager, First Nations Student Services | bdunn@csu.edu.au Closing Date: 11pm, 19 November 2026 Advertised: 29 Oct 2026 03:50 PM - AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 19 Nov 2026 11:00 PM - AUS Eastern Daylight Time Apply now Refer a friend Back to search results"
    },
    "https://external-jobs.csu.edu.au/cw/en/job/499144/digital-learning-officer": {
      "title": "Digital Learning Officer",
      "description": "Digital Learning Officer Apply now Reference no: 499144 Work type: Fixed Term - Full Time Location: Bathurst, Orange, Wagga Wagga Categories: Education and Training or Learning Support (i.e. Academic Skills, Educational Designers), Professional Create engaging online learning content Support academics using Brightspace and digital tools Combine creativity and technology to enhance student success. The role Full Time, Fixed Term (12 months) Level 6 - $95,246 to $103,022 p.a. (plus 17% superannuation) Bathurst, Orange or Wagga Wagga At Charles Sturt University, we're committed to delivering exceptional learning experiences for our students. As a Digital Learning Officer, you'll combine creativity, technology, and innovation to develop high-quality digital learning resources that enhance teaching, learning, and assessment across the university. You will partner with academic staff to develop high-quality digital learning experiences using the University’s Brightspace learning management system and a range of educational technologies. Working at the intersection of technology and teaching practice, you will help translate teaching intent into engaging, accessible, and effective online learning activities and resources. We are particularly interested in candidates with skills in audio-visual production, animation, or advanced interactive content development who can bring creative and technical expertise to the team. In this role, you will develop bespoke digital learning resources, build academic capability through practical guidance, and support innovative approaches to digital content development, including the appropriate use of emerging AI technologies. You will apply accessibility, copyright, brand, and quality standards, while managing competing priorities and delivering work to agreed timelines. You will also contribute to improving digital development practices and building relevant capability across the University. About you You enjoy working with people, solving problems, and creating digital content that makes a difference. You will bring: Experience developing high-quality digital content within an educational, training or learning environment. Knowledge of educational technology and online platforms The ability to manage tasks and meet deadlines Confidence supporting and training staff A strong focus on quality, accessibility, and clear design A collaborative mindset with a passion for innovation and continuous improvement. Experience in higher education (desirable, not essential) About us We are a university of the land and people of our regions. True to the character of regional Australia we have gumption, we have soul and we collaborate with others. We develop holistic, far-sighted people who help their communities grow and flourish. The Wiradjuri phrase yindyamarra winhanganha means the wisdom of respectfully knowing how to live well in a world worth living in. This phrase represents who we are at Charles Sturt University – our ethos. It comes from traditional Indigenous Australian knowledge, but it also speaks to the mission of universities – to develop and spread wisdom to make the world a better place. Harnessing technology, we thrive as a distributed yet connected community, welcoming and engaging with people across Australia and the world. Learn more about the Division of Learning and Teaching here . Visit our website to learn about our great range of employee benefits . Charles Sturt University is committed to building a diverse workforce as shown through our Athena Swan Bronze Award, our Australian Workplace Equality Index Bronze Award and our membership of a range of diversity focused organisations including Diversity Council Australia and Pride in Diversity. We encourage applications from First Nations people, carers, people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, people with disability, neurodivergent people, and people of all age groups, genders and sexualities for all roles. To apply Click the Apply button and complete your application online, submit a “statement of suitability” (maximum 1 page) demonstrating your skills, knowledge and/or experience relevant to the position (referencing the selection criteria in the position description) and upload your CV. If you experience difficulties applying online or for further information on completing the application process please visit our how to apply page or contact us . This position is open to Australian Citizens and Permanent Residents; or applicants who hold a current valid work visa commensurate with this position. Disclosure Requirement Applicants may be required to disclose information relating to gender-based violence allegations or findings as part of the University's recruitment and screening processes. Further information Additional information is available in the position description or by contacting: Recruitment specific enquiries: Nelvin Kumar | Talent Acquisition Consultant | nekumar@csu.edu.au Job specific enquiries: Ryun Fell | Manager, Educational Technologies | rfell@csu.edu.au Closing Date: 11pm, 12 November 2026 Advertised: 29 Oct 2026 09:00 AM - AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 12 Nov 2026 11:00 PM - AUS Eastern Daylight Time Apply now Refer a friend Back to search results"
    },
    "https://external-jobs.csu.edu.au/cw/en/job/499222/first-nations-strategic-relationships-and-development-lead-targeted-first-nations-position": {
      "title": "First Nations Strategic Relationships and Development Lead (Targeted First Nations Position)",
      "description": "First Nations Strategic Relationships and Development Lead (Targeted First Nations Position) Apply now Reference no: 499222 Work type: Continuing - Full Time Location: Bathurst Categories: Information Technology, Indigenous Targeted Grow your career and connect with mob at the university where you belong​ The role Full Time, Continuing Level 8 - $117,936 to $133,494 p.a. (plus 17% superannuation) Albury-Wodonga, Bathurst, Dubbo, Orange, Port Macquarie or Wagga Wagga As the First Nations Strategic Relationships and Development Lead, you will play a key role in strengthening and growing meaningful collaboration between the University, First Nations communities, organisations, government, and education sectors. You will identify and develop strategic opportunities that create positive outcomes for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, build collaborative relationships, and lead initiatives that contribute to community aspirations and organisational priorities. Working across a diverse network of stakeholders, you will champion engagement, facilitate partnerships, secure new opportunities through funding and business development activities, and help drive sustainable, long-term impact for First Nations outcomes. About you We are seeking an Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander professional who is passionate about creating opportunities for First Nations peoples and building strong, respectful relationships that deliver meaningful outcomes. You will bring: Strong connections to and understanding of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander communities, cultures and perspectives. Demonstrated ability to build and maintain trusted relationships with a diverse range of stakeholders. Experience identifying and developing strategic opportunities, partnerships or community-focused initiatives. Strong communication and engagement skills, with the ability to influence and collaborate across different sectors and communities. Experience preparing funding applications, business cases, grants, tenders or proposals. The ability to work independently while fostering collaboration and shared outcomes. A commitment to culturally safe, community-led approaches and creating positive change for First Nations peoples. Strategic thinking skills, coupled with the ability to turn ideas into practical and sustainable outcomes. About us At Charles Sturt University, we deeply value the knowledges, cultures and leadership that First Nations peoples bring. We're committed to building a culturally safe and inclusive workplace where mob feel respected, supported and connected. We're growing our First Nations workforce, with opportunities across teaching, research, student support and other professional roles. Bring your skills, experience and perspectives to meaningful work that supports our students, communities and future generations.​ When you join Charles Sturt, you'll be part of a connected First Nations staff community, with opportunities for professional development, cultural leave and connection through First Nations Staff Connect. We're committed to supporting First Nations careers and creating a workplace where mob feel valued, connected and supported to succeed.​ Learn more about the Office of Engagement and Enterprise here . Visit our website to learn about First Nations careers at Charles Sturt . Charles Sturt University is committed to building a diverse workforce as shown through our Athena Swan Bronze Award, our Australian Workplace Equality Index Bronze Award and our membership of a range of diversity focused organisations including Diversity Council Australia and Pride in Diversity. We encourage applications from First Nations people, carers, people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, people with disability, neurodivergent people, and people of all age groups, genders and sexualities for all roles. This position has been targeted for Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander Australians under Charles Sturt University’s Indigenous Employment Strategy. To apply Click the Apply button and complete your application online, submit a “statement of suitability” demonstrating your skills, knowledge and/or experience relevant to the position (referencing the selection criteria in the position description) and upload your CV. If you experience difficulties applying online or for further information on completing the application process please visit our how to apply page or contact us . Disclosure Requirement Applicants may be required to disclose information relating to gender-based violence allegations or findings as part of the University's recruitment and screening processes. Further information Additional information is available in the position description or by contacting: Amber Marks | Director, Business Development | amarks@csu.edu.au Closing Date: 11pm, 19 November 2026 Advertised: 29 Oct 2026 09:00 AM - AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 19 Nov 2026 11:00 PM - AUS Eastern Daylight Time Apply now Refer a friend Back to search results"
    },
    "https://external-jobs.csu.edu.au/cw/en/job/499223/events-and-communications-officer-first-nations-identified-first-nations-position": {
      "title": "Events and Communications Officer, First Nations - Identified First Nations Position",
      "description": "Events and Communications Officer, First Nations - Identified First Nations Position Apply now Reference no: 499223 Work type: Continuing - Full Time Location: Albury-Wodonga, Bathurst, Dubbo, Orange, Port Macquarie, Wagga Wagga Categories: Indigenous Identified, Indigenous Targeted Grow your career and connect with mob at the university where you belong​ The role Full Time, Continuing Level 5 - $84,869 to $92,654 p.a. (plus 17% superannuation) Albury-Wodonga, Bathurst, Dubbo, Orange, Port Macquarie or Wagga Wagga As the Events and Communications Officer, First Nations, you will play an important role in creating culturally safe, inclusive and engaging experiences for First Nations students, staff, communities and partners across Charles Sturt University. Working within First Nations Student Connect, you will coordinate and deliver a range of university events that celebrate achievement, foster connection and strengthen engagement with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples. You will also support First Nations communications activities, helping share stories, promote events and showcase the impact of our students, communities and programs. Working closely with colleagues across the University and external organisations, you will help ensure events and communications are culturally respectful, community-informed and aligned with the University's values and commitment to First Nations success. This role requires regular travel and occasional after-hours and weekend work. About you As an Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander person, you are passionate about supporting positive outcomes for First Nations students and communities and enjoy building strong relationships through culturally respectful engagement. You will bring: Knowledge and understanding of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander cultures, communities and contemporary issues, gained through lived experience, community connection and/or professional experience. Proven organisational skills with the ability to coordinate multiple activities and manage competing priorities. Strong communication and interpersonal skills, with the ability to build effective relationships across a broad range of stakeholders. Experience using Microsoft Office and event management, registration or administrative systems, or the ability to quickly learn new technologies. Experience with social media, digital communications, content creation or storytelling, or a willingness to develop these skills. A current unrestricted driver's licence. About us At Charles Sturt University, we deeply value the knowledges, cultures and leadership that First Nations peoples bring. We're committed to building a culturally safe and inclusive workplace where mob feel respected, supported and connected. We're growing our First Nations workforce, with opportunities across teaching, research, student support and other professional roles. Bring your skills, experience and perspectives to meaningful work that supports our students, communities and future generations.​ When you join Charles Sturt, you'll be part of a connected First Nations staff community, with opportunities for professional development, cultural leave and connection through First Nations Staff Connect. We're committed to supporting First Nations careers and creating a workplace where mob feel valued, connected and supported to succeed.​ Learn more about the Division of Student Success here . Visit our website to learn about First Nations careers at Charles Sturt . Charles Sturt University is committed to building a diverse workforce as shown through our Athena Swan Bronze Award, our Australian Workplace Equality Index Bronze Award and our membership of a range of diversity focused organisations including Diversity Council Australia and Pride in Diversity. We encourage applications from First Nations people, carers, people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, people with disability, neurodivergent people, and people of all age groups, genders and sexualities for all roles. This is an identified Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander position under section 14 of the NSW Anti-Discrimination Act, 1977. Please be aware you may be asked to provide confirmation of Indigenous Australian Identity. To apply Click the Apply button and complete your application online, submit a “statement of suitability” demonstrating your skills, knowledge and/or experience relevant to the position (referencing the selection criteria in the position description) and upload your CV. If you experience difficulties applying online or for further information on completing the application process please visit our how to apply page or contact us . Disclosure Requirement Applicants may be required to disclose information relating to gender-based violence allegations or findings as part of the University's recruitment and screening processes. Further information Additional information is available in the position description or by contacting: Melissa Olsen | Director, First Nations Student Connect | meolsen@csu.edu.au Closing Date: 11pm, 19 November 2026 Advertised: 29 Oct 2026 09:00 AM - AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 19 Nov 2026 11:00 PM - AUS Eastern Daylight Time Apply now Refer a friend Back to search results"
    },
    "https://external-jobs.csu.edu.au/jobs/appn-node-manager-wagga-wagga-nsw-australia-a166b011-2d8e-4cb4-a485-538df4f31b67": {
      "title": "APPN Node Manager",
      "description": "APPN Node Manager - Wagga Wagga, NSW, Australia Visit Charles Sturt University Existing Applicant? Sign up for jobs alerts My Profile Kiosk mode APPN Node Manager Wagga Wagga, NSW Continuing - Full Time Closing on: Nov 30 2026 Position Description Lead a nationally significant plant phenotyping research facility Drive major projects, partnerships and industry collaboration Lead a talented team and shape the future of agricultural innovation The role Full Time, Continuing Level 8 - $117,936 to $133,494 pa (plus 17% superannuation) Wagga Wagga The Australian Plant Phenomics Network (APPN) Node Manager will provide strategic and operational leadership for Charles Sturt University's APPN Node, based at the AgriPark in Wagga Wagga. This is an exciting opportunity to lead a multidisciplinary team of technical professionals, overseeing the delivery, development and ongoing operation of sophisticated plant phenotyping infrastructure, including the Rhizolysimeter, UAV and ground-based sensing platforms. You will drive operational excellence, build team capability and ensure the delivery of high-quality research outcomes through effective management of people, resources, facilities and data. A key focus of this role is developing strong relationships and driving collaboration across a diverse range of stakeholders, including researchers, industry partners, commercial innovators, government and national research infrastructure partners. You will take the initiative to identify opportunities, develop partnerships, grow facility utilisation and lead multiple complex projects from planning through to delivery, ensuring outcomes are achieved on time and within budget. With responsibility for budgets, business development, project delivery and the ongoing growth of the Node, you will play an important role in strengthening Charles Sturt University's contribution to agricultural research and innovation at a national level. About you Demonstrated experience leading, managing and developing multidisciplinary teams, with strong people management skills and the ability to motivate, mentor and build a high-performing, collaborative team culture. Highly developed stakeholder engagement and relationship management skills, with the ability to build productive partnerships, influence outcomes and confidently navigate competing priorities across diverse internal and external stakeholders. Proven experience leading and delivering multiple large-scale or complex projects, with strong project management capabilities and the ability to take ownership, show initiative and drive projects through to successful completion. Demonstrated experience in business development, partnership development and financial management, including identifying new opportunities, growing services or facilities, managing budgets and contributing to funding applications. Relevant technical expertise in plant phenotyping, remote and proximal sensing, imaging systems and data management, with the ability to provide technical leadership and support high-quality research outcomes. As part of this recruitment, a pre-employment functional screening is required. About us We are a university of the land and people of our regions. True to the character of regional Australia we have gumption, we have soul and we collaborate with others. We develop holistic, far-sighted people who help their communities grow and flourish. The Wiradjuri phrase yindyamarra winhanganha means the wisdom of respectfully knowing how to live well in a world worth living in. This phrase represents who we are at Charles Sturt University – our ethos. It comes from traditional Indigenous Australian knowledge, but it also speaks to the mission of universities – to develop and spread wisdom to make the world a better place. Harnessing technology, we thrive as a distributed yet connected community, welcoming and engaging with people across Australia and the world. Learn more about AgriPark here . Visit our website to learn about our great range of employee benefits . Charles Sturt University is committed to building a diverse workforce as shown through our Athena Swan Bronze Award, our Australian Workplace Equality Index Bronze Award and our membership of a range of diversity focused organisations including Diversity Council Australia and Pride in Diversity. We encourage applications from First Nations people, carers, people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, people with disability, neurodivergent people, and people of all age groups, genders and sexualities for all roles. To apply Click the Apply button and complete your application online, answering the below questions and upload your CV. If you experience difficulties applying online or for further information on completing the application process please visit our how to apply page or contact us . Describe a project where you worked in a multi-disciplinary team to design a project, attract funding, and deliver impact to industry? Where do you think the APPN can deliver the most value to the Australian agricultural industry? This position is open to Australian Citizens and Permanent Residents; or applicants who hold a current valid work visa commensurate with this position. Disclosure Requirement Applicants may be required to disclose information relating to gender-based violence allegations or findings as part of the University's recruitment and screening processes. Further information Additional information is available in the position description or by contacting: Recruitment specific enquiries: Tyesha Schein | Talent Acquisition Consultant  | tschein@csu.edu.au Job specific enquiries: Pip Grant | Executive Director, AgriPark| phgrant@csu.edu.au Closing Date: 11pm, 30 November 2026 Back to job search Apply Now First Name (required) 66c751bf Last Name (required) 8703180a Email (required) 7f2c8fb0 Refer someone to this job Apply Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Recommended Jobs Workplace Relations Specialist Albury-Wodonga, NSW. Bathurst, NSW. Dubbo, NSW. Orange, NSW. Other, NSW. Port Macquarie, NSW. Wagga Wagga, NSW Human Resources Shape the future of employee relations, drive positive change, lead strategic initiatives, and ensure compliance in a high-impact university role. Administrative Assistant Albury-Wodonga, NSW Administration, Child Care Provide essential administrative support in a welcoming childcare environment, helping deliver quality services to children and families. Project Manager, Graduate Research Albury-Wodonga, NSW. Bathurst, NSW. Orange, NSW. Port Macquarie, NSW. Wagga Wagga, NSW Project Management Lead strategic projects and initiatives that enhance the higher degree research experience, training and graduate research outcomes. Sign up for job alerts Job Alert df0f22a2 Finalize your job alert by selecting criteria from the dropdowns below. You can select multiple options from each dropdown by returning to the combobox and re-entering the list of options. Submit at the end to create your job alert. Categories Management Professional Management Professional Employment Type Continuing - Full Time Continuing - Full Time Locations Wagga Wagga, NSW, Australia Wagga Wagga, NSW, Australia First Name (required) 7e436ef2 Last Name (required) f0d706be Email (required) 1e3bbb72 Send Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you 'Yindyamarra winhanganha' The wisdom of respectfully knowing how to live well in a world worth living in We pay our respect to all First Nations elders both past and present from the lands where Charles Sturt University students reside. In particular, we acknowledge the Wiradjuri, Ngunawal, Gundungurra and Birpai peoples of Australia, who are the traditional custodians of the land where Charles Sturt University campuses are located. Stay connected Facebook X Youtube Instagram LinkedIn © 2026 Charles Sturt University - TEQSA Provider Identification: PRV12018 (Australian University). CRICOS Provider: 00005F. Accessibility Privacy Cookie Preferences Candidate Details We use cookies Some are necessary to operate the website and its functions. Others help personalise, improve content and services to show you the most relevant job opportunities. With the decision \"Accept essential only\" we will respect your privacy and will not set cookies that aren't necessary for the operation of the site. Accept essential only Accept all Please choose the location(s)"
