    "https://external-jobs.csu.edu.au/jobs/clinical-educator-dentistry-oral-health-casual-opportunity-bathurst-nsw-australia-dubbo-orange-wagga-wagga": {
      "title": "Clinical Educator (Dentistry / Oral Health) - Casual Opportunity",
      "description": "Clinical Educator  (Dentistry / Oral Health) - Casual Opportunity - Bathurst, NSW, Australia - Dubbo, NSW, Australia - Orange, NSW, Australia - Wagga Wagga, NSW, Australia Visit Charles Sturt University Existing Applicant? Sign up for jobs alerts My Profile Kiosk mode Clinical Educator  (Dentistry / Oral Health) - Casual Opportunity Bathurst, NSW Dubbo, NSW Orange, NSW Wagga Wagga, NSW Show More Show Less Casual Closing on: Nov 30 2026 Position Description Flexible casual hours to fit your schedule Opportunity to contribute to rural health education and make a difference in regional communities Work within a supportive team committed to excellence in teaching and clinical practice The Opportunity Casual / Sessional (on an as needs basis) Albury-Wodonga, Bathurst, Dubbo, Orange and Wagga Wagga Join Charles Sturt University as a Clinical Educator on a casual basis and play a pivotal role in shaping the future of dental and oral health professionals. Based in one of our regional Dental and Oral Health Clinics, you’ll provide hands-on clinical supervision and mentorship to students, bridging academic learning with real-world practice. This role supports the development of the dental workforce in rural and regional communities while offering opportunities for professional growth and academic engagement. About you Full registration as a dental practitioner with the Australian Health Practitioner Regulation Agency (Ahpra). Proven ability to empathise effectively and tailor teaching approaches to accommodate diverse learning styles while following the principles of good clinical supervision Willingness to adapt their teaching schedule as needed to facilitate collaboration with colleagues at varying times. A strong desire to contribute to advancing dental education, actively contributing to the institution’s educational goals, and deriving fulfilment from teaching and mentoring future dental professionals About us We are a university of the land and people of our regions. True to the character of regional Australia we have gumption, we have soul and we collaborate with others. We develop holistic, far-sighted people who help their communities grow and flourish. The Wiradjuri phrase yindyamarra winhanganha means the wisdom of respectfully knowing how to live well in a world worth living in. This phrase represents who we are at Charles Sturt University – our ethos. It comes from traditional Indigenous Australian knowledge, but it also speaks to the mission of universities – to develop and spread wisdom to make the world a better place. Harnessing technology, we thrive as a distributed yet connected community, welcoming and engaging with people across Australia and the world. Learn more about the Faculty of Science and Health here . Learn more about the School of Dentistry and Medical Sciences here . Charles Sturt University is committed to building a diverse workforce as shown through our Athena Swan Bronze Award, our Australian Workplace Equality Index Bronze Award and our membership of a range of diversity focused organisations including Diversity Council Australia and Pride in Diversity. We encourage applications from First Nations people, carers, people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, people with disability, neurodivergent people, and people of all age groups, genders and sexualities for all roles. To apply To express your interest for this role, please submit a cover letter that tells us more about you and attach your CV via email to ClinicalOps-DOHC@csu.edu.au Please note, registering your interest does not guarantee that an offer of employment will be made. This position is open to Australian Citizens and Permanent Residents; or applicants who hold a current valid work visa commensurate with this position. Further information Additional information is available in the position description or by contacting: Jessica Mullins| Acting Clinical Operations Coordinator| ClinicalOps-DOHC@csu.edu.au | Ph: (02) 6365 7070 Closing Date: 11pm, 30 November 2026 Note applications for this casual role is open until the closing date specified above, however applications will be reviewed on need basis before the closing date. Back to job search Recommended Jobs APPN Node Manager Wagga Wagga, NSW Management, Professional Lead a multidisciplinary team, drive major research projects and grow Australia's plant phenotyping capabilities. Workplace Relations Specialist Albury-Wodonga, NSW. Bathurst, NSW. Dubbo, NSW. Orange, NSW. Other, NSW. Port Macquarie, NSW. Wagga Wagga, NSW Human Resources Shape the future of employee relations, drive positive change, lead strategic initiatives, and ensure compliance in a high-impact university role. Administrative Assistant Albury-Wodonga, NSW Administration, Child Care Provide essential administrative support in a welcoming childcare environment, helping deliver quality services to children and families. Sign up for job alerts Job Alert c0d3748c Finalize your job alert by selecting criteria from the dropdowns below. You can select multiple options from each dropdown by returning to the combobox and re-entering the list of options. Submit at the end to create your job alert. Categories Dentistry Dentistry Employment Type Casual Casual Locations Bathurst, NSW, Australia Dubbo, NSW, Australia Orange, NSW, Australia Wagga Wagga, NSW, Australia Bathurst, NSW, Australia Dubbo, NSW, Australia Orange, NSW, Australia Wagga Wagga, NSW, Australia First Name (required) f3fa2759 Last Name (required) 584b584b Email (required) f06986ff Send Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you 'Yindyamarra winhanganha' The wisdom of respectfully knowing how to live well in a world worth living in We pay our respect to all First Nations elders both past and present from the lands where Charles Sturt University students reside. In particular, we acknowledge the Wiradjuri, Ngunawal, Gundungurra and Birpai peoples of Australia, who are the traditional custodians of 